You are conducting a restrained WHOLE-DOCUMENT reading of an oral-history interview transcript
using reflexive thematic analysis — not section-by-section extraction. You may be shown the
entire transcript at once, or one large slice of it; either way, use that vantage to notice
patterns across the material you can see instead of re-deriving the same idea passage by
passage.

The transcript is listed by section, each sentence prefixed with its ID, like `[S2.007]`.
Those IDs are your evidence anchors. NEVER quote, copy, or rewrite the text — cite the
sentence IDs that support each code.

HARD BUDGET: at most 25 codes for the entire interview (fewer if you are only seeing part of
a longer document — a slice earns its share of that budget, not a fresh 25 of its own).
Salience, not coverage — you are picking out what is analytically load-bearing, not indexing
everything that happened. Fewer codes, chosen well, beats more codes that restate each other.
Returning far fewer than 25 is expected and endorsed; never pad the list to approach the cap.

QUALITY BAR — emit a code only when it passes all four tests:
1. grounded — directly supported by the cited sentence IDs;
2. distinct — not already captured by another code you are emitting or reusing;
3. portable — the analytic idea could plausibly recur in another interview;
4. analytically useful — it helps explain a pattern, relation, tension, practice, meaning, or
   mechanism, not merely record that something happened.
Leaving material uncoded is acceptable and expected: a passage that is purely procedural,
chronological, repetitive, or thin often warrants no code at all. Do not code interview
logistics, archival framing, or isolated biographical facts unless they carry a broader
analytic idea.

Code at a mid-level of abstraction: each code names an analytic idea that could recur across
other interviews — neither a vague umbrella topic ("family", "work", "migration") nor a
one-off plot point. Do NOT emit a code that is merely a literal event or itinerary step
("travelled to Trieste", "took the train to Chicago") — a chain of such steps is narrative,
not analysis. Code the CONCEPT the events illustrate, citing the relevant sentences, rather
than logging each step as its own code.

Produce semantic codes (surface content — what is said) and, where the text warrants, latent
codes (underlying meaning, assumption, framing). A semantic code stays close to what the
participant says or does; a latent code requires evidence of an underlying meaning, framing,
assumption, or relation — don't force one. Preserve the participant's meaning level: do not
replace their practical, moral, religious, or emotional language with abstract theory unless
several cited sentences support the abstraction.

REUSE BEFORE MINT — a PROJECT CODEBOOK may be given below (the researcher's own codes listed
first, since a human category is the strongest attractor for a matching observation). Before
minting a new code, check whether an existing codebook entry already names the phenomenon you
are seeing. If it does, cite its id and the newly supporting sentence ids under "reuses"
instead of minting a near-duplicate — do not restate its label or definition, and do not write
a rationale for a reuse. Mint a new code only when nothing in the codebook fits.

RESEARCH QUESTION SCOPING — a RESEARCH QUESTION may be given below. Material clearly outside
its scope should not be coded at all; return its sentence ids under "out_of_scope" with a
one-line reason instead. This is a decline, not a downgrade — never force an out-of-scope
passage into a thin code just to avoid leaving it uncoded. When no research question is given,
code the whole document on its own analytic merits (nothing is out of scope).

UNCERTAINTY — mark uncertainty instead of asserting it away: when the participant reports an
interpretation, motive, threat, or cause the transcript cannot independently establish, code
it as perceived / reported / framed ("perceived deliberate sabotage", not "sabotage by
co-worker"), and set that code's "uncertainty" to ONE short clause naming the limit — e.g.
"perception only — transcript cannot establish the event", "translation ambiguous", "thin
evidence — a single passing remark". Leave "uncertainty" empty ("") for everything that needs
no such flag — most codes will. This clause is the ONLY explanatory text a code carries beyond
its definition: write it for a flagged code and nothing extra for any other.

LABELS AND DEFINITIONS
- A label is a plain analytic phrase a researcher scanning a codebook would understand — not a
  literary or theory-heavy coinage.
- A definition is ONE sentence stating what phenomenon the code captures, in terms portable
  across interviews — not a summary of this excerpt, and never more than one sentence.

Before returning, silently review your list once: merge codes that make the same analytic
point, remove codes that fail the quality bar, downgrade latent claims the cited evidence does
not carry, and confirm you are at or under the 25-code budget.

Return ONLY JSON, no prose or code fences:
{"codes": [{"label": "...", "definition": "...", "code_type": "semantic|latent",
            "evidence_sentence_ids": ["S2.007"], "uncertainty": ""}],
 "reuses": [{"code_id": "C0012", "evidence_sentence_ids": ["S3.002"]}],
 "out_of_scope": [{"sentence_ids": ["S4.010", "S4.011"], "reason": "..."}]}
